{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481944,"nonce":"3cuIwH54pUsWYg/ledkegW67+VE=","title":"**What is wrong.** `Service.observe` spans `internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go:147-440` — **294 lines**, 31% of a 936-line file — and it holds three distinct jobs in one body with no boundary between them. The `//nolint:gocyclo` at `:146` justifies it as *\"this IS the §B.3 table's driver; splitting it would scatter one decision across files.\"* That is a sound objection to moving the decision to another package, and no objection at all to giving it a function in the same file, which is what is missing.","message":"**The demonstration.** The three phases are already commented, already sequential, and share only a named accumulator:\n\n- **:151-191** — batch reads and the one upsert round trip: `priorAlerts`, `buildUpserts`, `UpsertBatch`, alert-id dedup, `latestOccurrences`, then the declaration of the five accumulators (`events`, `notifies`, `enrichIDs`, `outcomes`, `newEpisode`).\n- **:193-395** — the per-observation loop. 203 lines deciding one alert.\n- **:397-439** — the batch tail: the group-level `repeat interval elapsed` notify, `appendEvents`, `enqueueEnrich`, the §F.3 phase-ordering deferral, `enqueueNotify`, the result.\n\nThe loop body is the seam, and it is already written as one: both of its give-up paths are `outcomes = append(outcomes, out)` followed by `continue` (`:235-237` and `:297-299`), which is the shape of a function that returns early. Everything it needs is nameable — one `domain.Observation`, its `results[i]`, `prior`, the current `latest[alertID]`, `cfg`, `opt` — and everything it produces is either the `ObserveOutcome` or an append to the five accumulators.\n\nThe cost of the missing seam is visible in the file today. The two branches that open a new episode are near-duplicates: `:244-280` (T1/T7, no prior open episode) and `:303-329` (`r.OpensNewOccurrence`). Both call `openEpisode`, both append its events, both push to `enrichIDs`, both bump `newEpisode`, both set `out.OccurrenceOpened`, both handle the T10 auto-unack, both enqueue `reasonFired`. They have already drifted: the first enqueues a `reasonUnacked` notification alongside the auto-unack event (`:266-272`), the second appends the event and enqueues nothing (`:316-322`). Whether that asymmetry is deliberate is documented at neither site — and a reader cannot tell, because the two copies are 40 lines apart inside a 294-line function rather than one call with two arguments.\n\n**Why it matters.** CONTEXT.md §3 calls `AlertOccurrence` *\"One **contiguous firing episode** of an Alert\"* and CONTEXT.md §4 marks `alerts` as *\"Identity/dedup, the occurrence state machine, the append-only timeline. The heart.\"* This function is the only production path into that state machine, and it is currently only testable as a batch: to exercise one §B.3 edge you must construct an observation slice, a settings value, an options value, and stand up the four repositories the batch phases call. A per-observation seam makes each edge of the transition table addressable on its own, which is what the table deserves.\n\nThe other three oversized files in this module were reviewed and cleared as justified — `dispatch.go`, `alerts/domain/lifecycle.go`, `alerts/repository/alert.go` are all sectioned or hold a table plus its interpreter. This is the one that is genuinely a missing function rather than a large but navigable one.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go:146` (the nolint), `:147-440` (`observe`), `:193-395` (the loop that wants to be a method), `:244-280` and `:303-329` (the diverged duplicates), `:136-144` (the lock-order note any refactor must preserve).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** The per-observation decision is its own method on `Service` in the same file, taking one observation plus the batch-level reads and writing into a named accumulator type; the two open-episode branches call one helper, with the `reasonUnacked` asymmetry either removed or commented at the single site that now decides it; the `//nolint:gocyclo` is gone; the `alerts` lock-order note at `:136-144` is unchanged and still accurate; and a test drives individual §B.3 edges through the new method without assembling a batch.","files":null}]}